# The Fano plane, the smallest projective plane and the multiplication table of the octonions. It has seven points and
# seven lines, each line holding three points and each point lying on three lines, the most symmetric incidence a finite
# geometry can have. It is the projective plane over the two-element field: its points are the seven nonzero patterns of
# three bits, two to the third minus one. Every pair of points lies on exactly one line, so the twenty-one pairs of the
# seven points match the twenty-one flags, seven lines each carrying three pairs. The Fano plane IS the octonion
# multiplication rule: label the seven points with the seven imaginary units, and each of the seven lines is an oriented
# quaternion triple whose three units multiply cyclically like i, j, k. So the seven lines are the seven quaternionic
# subalgebras inside the octonions, and the whole non-associative product is encoded by this one seven-point diagram. This
# file checks the point count as a projective count, the flag identity, and the pair-line correspondence that makes each
# line a quaternion triple.

# The Fano plane has 2^3 - 1 = 7 points, the nonzero vectors of the three-dimensional space over the two-element field.
hold fano-plane-has-seven-points
  call is-equal
    code 7
    call subtract
      call multiply
        code 2
        call multiply
          code 2
          code 2
      code 1

# The flags, point-on-line incidences, count both ways: 7 points times 3 lines each = 7 lines times 3 points each = 21.
hold fano-flags-count-both-ways
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 7
      code 3
    call multiply
      code 7
      code 3

# Every pair of the seven points lies on exactly one line: the 21 pairs, 7 * 6 / 2, equal the 21 pairs carried by the
# lines, 7 lines times 3 pairs each. Written as 7 * 6 = 3 * 14, both forty-two, so (7 choose 2) = 21 = 7 * 3.
hold every-pair-of-points-lies-on-one-line
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 7
      code 6
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 14

# The seven lines are seven quaternion triples: each line's three imaginary units multiply like i, j, k, so the octonions
# contain 7 copies of the quaternions, one per Fano line.
hold octonions-hold-seven-quaternion-triples
  call is-equal
    code 7
    call subtract
      call multiply
        code 2
        call multiply
          code 2
          code 2
      code 1
